LD1 { Vt, Vt2 }, [Xn], #imm -- restore the pair post-index semantics.

`LD1_ASISDLSEP_I2_I2_*` had no live `DEF_ISEL`. The DECODER for it is fine and
symmetric with the store side (`TryDecodeLD1_ASISDLSEP_I2_I2` calls
`AddPostIndexMemOp` exactly as `TryDecodeST1_ASISDLSEP_I2_I2` does), so the
instruction decoded and then lifted to NOTHING.

❗ A missing DEF_ISEL here is SILENT, not fatal. `_ecv_unreached` fires only when
the decoder itself refuses; a decoded instruction with no semantics is simply a
no-op that neither loads its vectors nor advances its base register. Measured
2026-09-01 with `.agents-workspace/govm/ld1chk.c`: both destination vectors stay
zero and the base register does not move.

What that cost: Go's `internal/bytealg.indexbytebody` computes its return value
FROM the advanced pointer --

    121d4: ld1 {v1.16b-v2.16b}, [x3], #32
    12230: sub x3, x3, #0x20
    1223c: sub x0, x0, x11          ; index = advanced - original

-- so with the load a no-op every `IndexByte` returned a negative index. glibc's
`gostringnocopy`/`findnull` path then built a Go string of length -32 and
`runtime.printFuncName` looped forever, which is why `postgres:17` hung after
`exec gosu` rather than printing a traceback.

The writeback is NOT performed by the semantics. `AddPostIndexMemOp` carries it
on the memory operand, which is why `ST1_PAIR_POSTINDEX` simply delegates to
`ST1_PAIR`; this mirrors that exactly.

⚠️ The dead code that was there could NOT be uncommented. It was written against
the older `DEF_SEM(..., R64 addr_reg, ADDR next_addr)` signature and did its own
`Write(addr_reg, Read(next_addr))`, which under the current convention would
update the base register a SECOND time.

⚠️ Both arms of the `#if defined(__x86_64__)` split are covered; the two branches
name their pair helpers differently (`LD1_PAIR_8_128` vs `LD1_PAIR_8`), so the
ISELs cannot be shared.

Still missing after this patch, and deliberately out of scope: the 3- and
4-register LD1 forms (they need `TTriple`/`TQuad` return types that do not
exist), and five ST1 forms that have no `TryDecode*` at all and therefore fail
LOUDLY rather than silently. See `.agents/docs/TODO.md`.

